input.prompt:
Repository: <workspace>

Task: Fix the Set-Cookie merge bug in Hono's `Context.res` setter (`src/context.ts`), and add regression tests in `src/context.test.ts`.

Bug: In `set res(_res)`, when `this.#res` already has a `set-cookie` header and the newly assigned `_res` also has its own `set-cookie` header, the current code does:

```ts
if (k === 'set-cookie') {
  const cookies = this.#res.headers.getSetCookie()
  _res.headers.delete('set-cookie')
  for (const cookie of cookies) {
    _res.headers.append('set-cookie', cookie)
  }
}
```

It deletes the new response's own cookies, so only the context's cookies survive. Required behavior: both the context (middleware) cookie and the new Response (handler) cookie must survive.

Exact required implementation in `src/context.ts` setter. Replace the existing setter body with:

```ts
  set res(_res: Response | undefined) {
    if (this.#res && _res) {
      _res = createResponseInstance(_res.body, _res)
      for (const [k, v] of this.#res.headers.entries()) {
        if (k === 'content-type' || k === 'set-cookie') {
          continue
        }
        _res.headers.set(k, v)
      }
      const contextCookies = this.#res.headers.getSetCookie()
      if (contextCookies.length > 0) {
        const contextCookieSet = new Set(contextCookies)
        const responseCookies = _res.headers.getSetCookie()
        _res.headers.delete('set-cookie')
        for (const cookie of contextCookies) {
          _res.headers.append('set-cookie', cookie)
        }
        for (const cookie of responseCookies) {
          // `_res` may already contain the context cookies when it was created
          // via `c.text()`, `c.json()`, etc. Avoid duplicating those cookies.
          if (!contextCookieSet.has(cookie)) {
            _res.headers.append('set-cookie', cookie)
          }
        }
      }
    }
    this.#res = _res
    this.finalized = true
  }
```

Rationale: iterating set-cookie per-entry is avoided (entries() yields one entry per cookie in Node/undici), context cookies are preserved first, and the new response's own cookies are kept after them. Exact duplicates already inherited from the context are not duplicated.

Test changes in `src/context.test.ts`:

1) Update the existing test `'Should set cookie headers when re-assigning Response to `c.res`'` (around line 430). After the fix, reassigning a Response with its own cookie yields context cookies first then the new response cookie. Replace the final two assertions:

```ts
    expect(c.res.headers.getSetCookie().length).toBe(3)
    expect(c.res.headers.getSetCookie()).toEqual([...cookies, ...newCookies])
```

2) Add these two regression tests immediately after that updated test, inside the `describe('Context header', ...)` block:

```ts
  it('Should keep both the context cookie and the response Set-Cookie header when re-assigning `c.res`', () => {
    // Middleware sets a cookie on `c.res` before the handler runs.
    c.res.headers.append('set-cookie', 'middleware=1; Path=/')

    // The handler returns a new Response with its own Set-Cookie header.
    const handlerResponse = new Response('ok', {
      headers: [['set-cookie', 'handler=1; Path=/']],
    })
    c.res = handlerResponse

    const cookies = c.res.headers.getSetCookie()
    expect(cookies).toContain('middleware=1; Path=/')
    expect(cookies).toContain('handler=1; Path=/')
    expect(cookies.length).toBe(2)
  })

  it('Should not duplicate a cookie already inherited from `c.res`', () => {
    c.res.headers.append('set-cookie', 'foo=bar; Path=/')
    c.res = c.text('Hi')
    expect(c.res.headers.getSetCookie()).toEqual(['foo=bar; Path=/'])
  })
```

Constraints:
- Only edit `src/context.ts` and `src/context.test.ts`. Do not modify any other file.
- Do not run any git command that stages or commits.
- Do not change unrelated formatting.
- After editing, run `pnpm exec vp test --run src/context.test.ts` and report the exact pass/fail summary. If anything fails, fix within the same two files only.
- Report back with the exact diff (`git diff -- src/context.ts src/context.test.ts`) and the test summary.

